text:
**Refined prompt:** Design — not yet implement — how to give comments an `author` in this NestJS/TypeORM RealWorld API: the entity relation, the write path, the response boundary, and the `DELETE /comments/:id` ownership check it exists to unblock. Answering as a maintainer bound by this repo's conventions (RO envelope, nested request bodies, controller→service→repository, mirror the nearest sibling) and by LESSONS.md. Output: a design with cited `file:line`, explicit decision points, and named risks. · Assumptions: targets the TypeORM implementation, not `prisma/` (CURRENT_FOCUS.md:23-24).

This is the blocker named in CURRENT_FOCUS.md:10-12 — the comment ownership check "needs a `comment.author` relation that does not exist yet."

## Where things stand

| Piece | Today |
|---|---|
| Entity | `comment.entity.ts:1-15` — `id`, `body`, `@ManyToOne article`. No author, no timestamps. |
| Write | `article.service.ts:101-112` `addComment(slug, commentData)` — never sees a user; `article.controller.ts:76-79` has no `@User()` param. |
| Read | `article.service.ts:165-168` returns `article.comments` raw (eager per `article.entity.ts:40`). |
| Response type | `article.interface.ts:3-5` — a local `interface Comment { body: string }` that shadows the entity class name and understates what actually serializes. |
| Ownership | None, and nothing to compare against (auth.md:60). |

The route is already guarded (`article.module.ts:28`), and `ArticleService` already injects `followsRepository` (`article.service.ts:22-23`) with `FollowsEntity` registered in `article.module.ts:13` — so no module wiring changes.

## Design

### 1. Entity — `comment.entity.ts`

```ts
@ManyToOne(type => UserEntity, { nullable: true })
author: UserEntity;
```

Three deliberate choices:

**`nullable: true` is effectively forced.** `synchronize: true` with no migration files (workspace.md:22, schema.md:8-9) means TypeORM ALTERs the live `comment` table at boot. A NOT NULL FK added to a table with existing rows gets an implicit `0`, and the FK constraint to a nonexistent `user` 0 then fails — boot error. There is no migration hook to backfill in, so nullable + null-safe handling at the boundary is the only path that doesn't require dropping the table.

**Unidirectional — no inverse side on `UserEntity`.** `ArticleEntity.author` declares one (`user => user.articles`, `article.entity.ts:37`), but nothing here needs `user.comments`, and omitting it avoids touching `UserEntity` and adding another circular import. Cheap to add later.

**Not eager.** This is the important one. `ArticleEntity.comments` is already `{eager: true}` (`article.entity.ts:40`), and TypeORM's `find*` methods resolve eager relations recursively — so `eager: true` on `Comment.author` would attach a full `UserEntity`, **including the argon2 `password` column** (`user.entity.ts:25-26`), to every comment on every article load: `findOne`, `addComment`, `deleteComment`, `favorite`, `unFavorite`, `update`. `GET /articles/:slug` and `GET /articles/:slug/comments` are public (auth.md:45-47). `ProfileService.findOne` has to `delete user.password` by hand (`profile.service.ts:25-26`) precisely because nothing else strips it. Keeping the relation lazy means the embedded-comment paths keep returning `author: undefined` rather than a password hash, and only the two paths with a real boundary mapper load it.

(`findAll` uses QueryBuilder — `article.service.ts:28-30` — which ignores eager relations entirely, so it is unaffected either way.)

### 2. Write path

Controller gains the identity param; service gains the parameter and one assignment:

```ts
// article.controller.ts
async createComment(@User('id') userId: number, @Param('slug') slug, @Body('comment') commentData: CreateCommentDto)

// article.service.ts — addComment(userId, slug, commentData)
comment.author = await this.userRepository.findOne(userId);
```

The assignment must land **before** `commentRepository.save(comment)` at `article.service.ts:109` — that save inserts the row standalone, so an author set afterwards never reaches the DB.

`CreateCommentDto` (`dto/create-comment.ts`) stays `{ body }` — do **not** add `author`/`authorId`. Identity comes from the token, never the body. `addComment` copies by name today (`article.service.ts:105`) rather than `Object.assign`, so it is already safe per LESSONS.md #112; the note matters because an `authorId` field in the DTO would turn the first future `Object.assign` into author spoofing.

### 3. Response boundary — `article.interface.ts`

Replace the shadowing `interface Comment` (`:3-5`):

```ts
import { ProfileData } from '../profile/profile.interface';

export interface CommentData {
  id: number;
  createdAt: Date;
  updatedAt: Date;
  body: string;
  author: ProfileData;
}

export interface CommentRO  { comment: CommentData; }
export interface CommentsRO { comments: CommentData[]; }
```

`author` is `ProfileData` (`profile.interface.ts:1-6`) — `{username, bio, image, following}` — not `UserData`, which carries `token` and `email`. A private mapper on `ArticleService` (controllers do no business logic, workspace.md:56-58) converts entity → `CommentData`, with `following` set only when a caller id is known, mirroring `ProfileService.findProfile`'s `if (id)` guard (`profile.service.ts:43-45`).

**Batch the follow lookup.** `findProfile` does one `followsRepository.findOne` per profile; applied per comment that is N+1 on a public endpoint. Instead: one `followsRepository.find({followerId: currentUserId})` for the whole list, into a `Set` of `followingId`.

`findComments` loads the relation explicitly rather than relying on eager:

```ts
const article = await this.articleRepository.findOne({
  where: { slug },
  relations: ['comments', 'comments.author'],
});
```

LESSONS.md's rule is to be able to name the loader for any relation you touch — this is that line.

### 4. What it unblocks — the ownership check

```ts
async deleteComment(userId: number, slug: string, id: string): Promise<ArticleRO> {
  const comment = await this.commentRepository.findOne(id, { relations: ['author'] });
  if (!comment) throw new HttpException('Comment not found.', HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND);
  if (!comment.author || comment.author.id !== userId) {
    throw new HttpException('You are not the author of this comment.', HttpStatus.FORBIDDEN);
  }
  // ...existing splice/delete
}
```

Two things worth being explicit about. The `{ relations: ['author'] }` is load-bearing — without it `comment.author` is `undefined` and `.id` throws a 500, which is the exact shape of the `user.favorites.findIndex` bug in LESSONS.md. And `!comment.author` — pre-existing authorless rows — must **deny**: the null case is "unknown owner," and defaulting it to allow would make every legacy comment deletable by anyone. Today's `deleteComment` (`article.service.ts:114-129`) also silently returns `{article}` when the id isn't found; the 404 above replaces that.

This also makes the `@ApiResponse({ status: 403 })` on `article.controller.ts:83` true for the first time (auth.md:64-66).

## Risks

**A new 500 surface on a public route.** `GET /articles/:slug/comments` is unguarded (auth.md:45-47) and currently never touches a token. Adding `@User('id')` to compute `following` routes it through the decorator's bare `jwt.verify` (`user.decorator.ts:15`), which throws on an expired or tampered token with nothing catching it → 500 instead of 401 (LESSONS.md #79, auth.md:35). A 60-day expiry (auth.md:11) makes stale tokens routine. Either fix the decorator's `verify` in this change, or accept that this endpoint newly inherits the bug — but decide it, don't discover it.

**Comments embedded in article responses stay authorless.** `addComment`, `deleteComment`, `findOne`, `favorite`, `unFavorite` and `update` all return `{article}` with eagerly-loaded comments as raw entities. Those keep serializing `author: undefined`. That is not a regression (today there is no author at all), and the fix is the article-response mapper already queued in CURRENT_FOCUS.md:7-9 — but per LESSONS.md #99, a spec field that is declared and not produced is the bug, so it should be written down rather than assumed.

**Adjacent, not fixed here:** `ArticleData.author?: UserData` (`article.interface.ts:17`) has the same wrong type — should be `ProfileData`. One line for the spec pass.

## Decision points

1. **Comment timestamps.** The spec's comment object has `createdAt`/`updatedAt`; the entity has neither. *Recommend including* — the entity and the mapper are both open in this change, and unlike `ArticleEntity.created`/`updated` (misnamed, queued for boundary renaming) a new entity can just be named correctly. `default: CURRENT_TIMESTAMP` handles existing rows cleanly. Skipping it means `CommentData` ships with fields nothing produces.
2. **`POST /articles/:slug/comments` envelope.** Returns `{article}` today (`article.service.ts:111`); the spec says `{comment}`. This is LESSONS.md #57's exact failure mode — a RealWorld frontend reading `response.comment.body` gets `undefined`. *Recommend switching* to `CommentRO` now that the mapper exists, but it is a visible behavior change and cleanly separable if you want it on its own commit.
3. **`password` as `select: false`.** The real fix for the leak class, but it breaks login (`UserService.findOne` compares the hash) without an `addSelect`. Flagging, not recommending, inside this change.

## Files touched

`comment.entity.ts` (author + timestamps) → `article.interface.ts` (CommentData/RO) → `article.service.ts` (addComment, findComments, deleteComment) → `article.controller.ts` (`@User('id')` on the three handlers, Swagger 403/404). No module changes.

**Verification is manual.** The only spec in the repo is `tag/tag.controller.spec.ts` (workspace.md:28) — there is no article-module test to update and no harness to add one to cheaply. Worth exercising by hand: comment as user A, delete as user B (expect 403), delete as A (expect success), `GET .../comments` with and without a token (`following` present vs absent), and an article read to confirm no `password` appears anywhere.

**Not read:** `prisma/` (out of scope per CURRENT_FOCUS.md:23-24), `user.service.ts`, `auth.middleware.ts`, `main.ts`, the tag module. Middleware and decorator behavior above is from `.claude/knowledge/auth.md` except `user.decorator.ts`, which I read directly.
